nt\": fixed_point, \"text\": text_final}.\n\nNow write the Python source.\n\nWe must ensure no mutation of case. We'll make a copy of text.\n\nThe Python source must be JSON-encoded as string inside the \"implementation\" key. So we produce JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in text:\\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\\n                applications.append(p['name'])\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': True, 'text': text}\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': False, 'text': text}\\n\"}\n\nBut watch out: The output contract says \"fixed_point\" boolean. Good.\n\nWe need to ensure we escape newlines and quotes properly. In JSON string, newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, quotes become \\\". We'll write the source as a string literal with proper escaping. Alternatively, we can construct the JSON object using json.dumps. But we are to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we can write the entire response as a JSON object string manually or use code to generate. Since we are writing the response directly, we must ensure correct escaping. I'll write the source with proper escapes.\n\nThe source code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in text:\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changed = True\n        if not